<!-- PART VIII -->
<div class="part-header">
  <p class="part-label">Part VIII</p>
  <h2>The Single Market &amp; Balassa's Six Levels</h2>
  <p class="part-desc">Regional integration theory — and where the EU currently stands</p>
</div>

<h2>Balassa's Six Levels of Regional Integration</h2>
<p>The Hungarian economist <strong>Béla Balassa</strong> (1961) described six progressive stages of economic integration. Each level is cumulative — it includes all features of previous levels.</p>

<div class="level-card">
  <div class="level-card-header"><span class="level-num">1</span> Free Trade Area</div>
  <div class="level-card-body"><strong>What it adds:</strong> Eliminates tariffs and quotas between members. Each country keeps its own tariff policy toward the rest of the world.<br><strong>Example:</strong> NAFTA/USMCA (USA, Canada, Mexico).</div>
</div>

<div class="level-card">
  <div class="level-card-header"><span class="level-num">2</span> Customs Union</div>
  <div class="level-card-body"><strong>What it adds:</strong> A <strong>common external tariff (CET)</strong> — the same duty on goods entering from outside, regardless of which member country they pass through. Eliminates tariff shopping.<br><strong>EU milestone:</strong> Completed in 1968. The Common Customs Tariff is now an exclusive EU competence.</div>
</div>

<div class="level-card">
  <div class="level-card-header"><span class="level-num">3</span> Common Market (Single Market)</div>
  <div class="level-card-body"><strong>What it adds:</strong> Free movement of the four factors: goods (Art. 34-36 TFEU), services (Art. 56-62), capital (Art. 63-66), and persons (Art. 45-55). Requires legal harmonization and mutual recognition.<br><strong>EU milestone:</strong> Formally completed 1 January 1993.</div>
</div>

<div class="level-card">
  <div class="level-card-header"><span class="level-num">4</span> Economic Union</div>
  <div class="level-card-body"><strong>What it adds:</strong> Harmonized economic policies — fiscal coordination, social policy alignment, regulatory frameworks. Member states coordinate budgets and economic governance.<br><strong>EU:</strong> Partially achieved — the Stability and Growth Pact requires deficits below 3% of GDP.</div>
</div>

<div class="level-card">
  <div class="level-card-header"><span class="level-num">5</span> Economic and Monetary Union (EMU)</div>
  <div class="level-card-body"><strong>What it adds:</strong> A single currency + a single central bank setting monetary policy for all members. Exchange rates between members disappear permanently.<br><strong>EU:</strong> Achieved for the 20 Eurozone countries — the ECB sets one interest rate for all.</div>
</div>

<div class="eu-position">⭐ THE EU IS CURRENTLY AT LEVEL 5 — Economic and Monetary Union ⭐</div>

<div class="level-card">
  <div class="level-card-header"><span class="level-num">6</span> Political Union</div>
  <div class="level-card-body"><strong>What it adds:</strong> Full transfer of sovereignty — common government, foreign policy, defense, tax system. Member states become like federated states.<br><strong>EU:</strong> Not yet achieved. Member states retain fundamental sovereignty over their governments, constitutions, and armies. Whether the EU should move toward this remains the most contested question in European politics.</div>
</div>

<!-- PART IX -->
<div class="part-header">
  <p class="part-label">Part IX</p>
  <h2>EU Trade Policy</h2>
  <p class="part-desc">The Common Customs Tariff, CETA, TTIP, and MERCOSUR</p>
</div>

<p>Trade policy is an <strong>exclusive EU competence</strong>. Individual member states cannot negotiate trade agreements with third countries — the Commission does that for all 27. This gives the EU enormous leverage: it speaks for 450 million consumers and the world's largest single market.</p>

<table>
  <tr><th>Agreement</th><th>Partner</th><th>Status</th><th>Key features</th></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>CCT</strong></td><td>All non-EU countries</td><td>In force</td><td>Uniform tariff on all non-EU imports. Revenue goes to EU budget. Exclusive EU competence.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>CETA</strong></td><td>Canada</td><td>Provisionally in force (2017)</td><td>"New generation" treaty — removes 99% of tariffs, covers services, investment, IP, public procurement.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>TTIP</strong></td><td>USA</td><td>Negotiations stalled (2016)</td><td>Ambitious EU-US free trade area — paused over regulatory standards disputes and political opposition.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>MERCOSUR</strong></td><td>Argentina, Brazil, Paraguay, Uruguay</td><td>Political deal (2019), not yet in force</td><td>World's largest FTA by population. Blocked by EU farmers (competition fears) and environmental concerns (deforestation).</td></tr>
</table>

<!-- PART X -->
<div class="part-header">
  <p class="part-label">Part X</p>
  <h2>Competition Law</h2>
  <p class="part-desc">Articles 101 and 102 TFEU, State Aid — with landmark cases and fines</p>
</div>

<div class="article-box">
  <h4>Article 101 TFEU — Anti-Competitive Agreements</h4>
  <p>Prohibits agreements between companies that restrict or distort competition: price-fixing cartels, market sharing, production limits, bid rigging. Such agreements are void. Fines up to <strong>10% of global annual turnover</strong>. Exception (Art. 101(3)): if the agreement improves production, benefits consumers, and does not eliminate competition.</p>
</div>

<div class="box box-example">
  <p class="box-title">Real Case — Truck Cartel (2016)</p>
  <p>Daimler, Volvo/Renault, Iveco, and DAF secretly fixed truck prices for <strong>14 years</strong> and delayed introduction of cleaner emission technologies. Commission fine: <strong>€2.93 billion</strong> — one of the largest cartel fines in EU history.</p>
</div>

<div class="article-box">
  <h4>Article 102 TFEU — Abuse of Dominant Position</h4>
  <p>Being dominant is not illegal — <strong>abusing</strong> that dominance is. Dominant = roughly 40%+ market share (case-by-case). Abusive practices: predatory pricing (selling below cost to eliminate rivals), exclusivity arrangements, tying/bundling, margin squeeze.</p>
</div>

<div class="box box-example">
  <p class="box-title">Real Case — Google Android (2018)</p>
  <p>Google required Android smartphone manufacturers to pre-install Google Search and Chrome, and prohibited alternative Android versions. This foreclosed rivals from the search and browser market. Commission fine: <strong>€4.34 billion</strong> — largest competition fine in EU history at the time.</p>
</div>

<div class="article-box">
  <h4>Article 107 TFEU — State Aid</h4>
  <p>Government subsidies, tax breaks, or favorable loans granted to specific companies that distort competition are generally prohibited. States must notify the Commission before granting aid. Exceptions: regional development, R&amp;D, natural disasters, public services in remote areas.</p>
</div>

<div class="box box-example">
  <p class="box-title">Real Case — Apple/Ireland (2016)</p>
  <p>Ireland allowed Apple to pay an effective corporate tax rate as low as <strong>0.005%</strong> in some years — via special tax rulings constituting illegal state aid. The Commission ordered Apple to repay <strong>€13 billion</strong> to Ireland (which Ireland initially appealed against).</p>
</div>

<!-- PART XI -->
<div class="part-header">
  <p class="part-label">Part XI</p>
  <h2>Key Business Regulations</h2>
  <p class="part-desc">GDPR, Digital Markets Act, AI Act, CSRD — how the EU shapes business today</p>
</div>

<table>
  <tr><th>Regulation / Directive</th><th>Year</th><th>What it does</th><th>Business impact</th></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>GDPR</strong></td><td>2018</td><td>Unified data protection law across EU. Applies to any company processing EU residents' data — worldwide.</td><td>Fines up to €20M or <strong>4% of global annual turnover</strong>. Must appoint DPO for high-risk processing. Data breaches: 72h notification.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>Digital Markets Act</strong></td><td>2022</td><td>Designates large platforms as "gatekeepers" (Google, Apple, Meta, Amazon, Microsoft) and imposes obligations.</td><td>Must allow third-party app stores, not self-preference, share data, ensure interoperability. Fines: <strong>10% global turnover</strong> (20% repeat).</td></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>AI Act</strong></td><td>2024</td><td>Risk-based AI regulation: unacceptable risk (banned), high risk (strict requirements), limited risk (transparency), minimal risk (no obligations).</td><td>High-risk AI (hiring, credit, medical) requires conformity assessment before market. World's first comprehensive AI law.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>CSRD</strong></td><td>2024</td><td>Mandatory ESG reporting for large listed companies — carbon, supply chain, gender pay gap, board diversity.</td><td>Must be verified by independent auditor, like financial accounts. Extends non-financial disclosure obligations significantly.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>USB-C Directive</strong></td><td>2022</td><td>Universal charger standard for electronic devices.</td><td>All devices must use USB-C from 2024. Example of product harmonization via directive.</td></tr>
</table>

<!-- PART XII -->
<div class="part-header">
  <p class="part-label">Part XII</p>
  <h2>The Charter of Fundamental Rights</h2>
  <p class="part-desc">Six titles, legally binding since the Lisbon Treaty (2009)</p>
</div>

<p>The <strong>Charter of Fundamental Rights of the EU</strong> was proclaimed in 2000 and became <strong>legally binding</strong> with the Treaty of Lisbon in 2009. It applies to EU institutions at all times, and to member states <em>when implementing EU law</em>.</p>

<table>
  <tr><th>Title</th><th>Articles</th><th>Key rights covered</th></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>I — Dignity</strong></td><td>1–5</td><td>Human dignity, right to life, prohibition of torture, prohibition of slavery and forced labour</td></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>II — Freedoms</strong></td><td>6–19</td><td>Right to liberty, respect for private life, freedom of thought, religion, expression, right to education, right to property, right to asylum</td></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>III — Equality</strong></td><td>20–26</td><td>Equality before the law, non-discrimination, cultural diversity, gender equality, rights of the child, elderly, persons with disabilities</td></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>IV — Solidarity</strong></td><td>27–38</td><td>Workers' right to information, right to collective bargaining and strike, fair working conditions, social security, healthcare</td></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>V — Citizens' Rights</strong></td><td>39–46</td><td>Right to vote in EU elections, petition Parliament, right to good administration, right of access to documents</td></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>VI — Justice</strong></td><td>47–50</td><td>Right to effective remedy, fair trial, presumption of innocence, ne bis in idem (cannot be tried twice for the same offence)</td></tr>
</table>

<div class="box box-warning">
  <p class="box-title">⚠ ECHR vs EU Charter — Do Not Confuse</p>
  <p><strong>European Convention on Human Rights (ECHR)</strong> = Council of Europe instrument, applies to 46 states, enforced by the ECtHR in Strasbourg.<br>
  <strong>EU Charter of Fundamental Rights</strong> = EU law, applies to 27 EU states when acting within EU law scope, part of primary EU law.<br>
  Two completely different systems, both operating in parallel.</p>
</div>

<!-- CLOSING -->
<div class="part-header" style="page-break-before: always;">
  <p class="part-label">Summary</p>
  <h2>The Essential Framework</h2>
  <p class="part-desc">Three foundations to remember from Chapter 1</p>
</div>

<p><strong>Historically</strong>, the EU is a peace project born from two World Wars, which evolved step by step from a coal and steel community (1951) into the world's most advanced form of regional integration — now at Balassa's Level 5 (Economic and Monetary Union).</p>

<p><strong>Institutionally</strong>, it is governed by a carefully balanced architecture: the <em>Commission</em> proposes and enforces, the <em>Parliament</em> and <em>Council</em> co-legislate, the <em>European Council</em> sets direction, the <em>ECB</em> manages the euro, and the <em>CJEU</em> ensures the law is respected — each checking the others.</p>

<p><strong>Legally</strong>, it operates through a unique legal order that is supreme over national law (Costa v ENEL), directly invocable by individuals (Van Gend en Loos), and built on a hierarchy from founding treaties (TEU/TFEU) down through regulations, directives, and decisions — governed by the principles of conferral, subsidiarity, and proportionality.</p>

